# local-failover-heavy — the stock-refusal Ollama tier. Two ways in:
#   1. Fallback — src/proxy/mlx_admin.py sends a request here when the MLX
#      server backing qwen38-action will not start. Bare-mode stripping
#      has already happened by then.
#   2. Deliberate — `/model qwen-stock`, for when you want a model whose refusal
#      behaviour is intact. The route path strips via ROUTE_BARE below.
#
# `/model qwen` and the `qwen` wrapper's default/lean mode no longer come here.
# They normally go to local-qwen38-obliterated; see that profile for the
# decision and its date.
#
# Two wrapper modes have never come here:
#   `qwen full` — full mode exists to carry the harness (~29K tokens). Stays on
#     the 4B @ 64K; see local-qwen35.env and the 2026-07-09 prefill revert.
#   `qwen fast` — "fast" means fast. Stays on the 4B (local-fast.env).
#
# 2026-08-25: this tier was qwen3.8:27b-bare (GGUF, 17GB resident). That tag was
# REMOVED from this host to free disk for the MLX artifact in
# local-qwen38-obliterated.env, which is now the default brain and failover tier.
# This profile kept its name and its job description, but it has been demoted:
# it is the FALLBACK the router uses when the MLX server will not start, and the
# tier `/model qwen-stock` names when you want refusal behaviour intact.
#
#   * Restoring the 27B GGUF is possible but not free: `ollama pull qwen3.8:27b`
#     is a 16.81GB single-blob transfer, then rebuild the bare tag from
#     modelfiles/bare/qwen3.8-27b-bare.Modelfile. That Modelfile is kept for
#     exactly this reason. It also needs Ollama >= 0.32; see its post-mortem.
#   * Do not delete this profile because "qwen points somewhere else now".
#     src/proxy/mlx_admin.py names it as MLX_FALLBACK_PROFILE, and a failover
#     with no fallback is the outage this whole subsystem exists to prevent.
#
#   qwen3.5:9b-64k — GGUF, ~10-12GB loaded at OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL=2 (q8_0 KV +
#   flash attention). Same tag as local-qwen-9b.env, different route semantics:
#   that profile is unstripped scoped-subagent work, this one is bare-mode
#   failover. Keep them separate — adding ROUTE_BARE to local-qwen-9b would
#   strip tool definitions out from under the fusion agent.
#
# TOOL-CAPABLE, and that is why it is qwen and not deepseek. This tier is paired
# with `failover_keep_tools=local`, which passes Read/Edit/Bash/Glob/Grep
# through so the model can keep doing work offline. deepseek-r1 cannot: Ollama
# rejects any request carrying tool definitions with HTTP 400 ("does not support
# tools"), which kills the session failover exists to save. If this tier is ever
# swapped for a model without tool support, set failover_keep_tools="" too.
#
# REASONING_EFFORT=none suppresses thinking traces for latency. Verified
# 2026-08-06 on qwen3.5 that this does NOT break tool calling (both settings
# emitted a correct get_weather call).
#
# Test the /v1 path, not just /api/chat: `reasoning_effort` as a top-level
# /api/chat argument did NOT suppress thinking here, so checking only that
# endpoint would have reported a false failure. PROVIDER_BASE_URL below is /v1
# for a reason.
#
# Re-run after the next Ollama or model upgrade; a tier that stops calling tools
# fails quietly, and failover keeps looking healthy until the model has to
# actually do something:
#   curl -s localhost:11434/v1/chat/completions -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#     -d '{"model":"qwen3.5:9b-64k","reasoning_effort":"none",
#          "messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Weather in Paris? Use the tool."}],
#          "tools":[{"type":"function","function":{"name":"get_weather",
#            "parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"city":{"type":"string"}}}}}]}' \
#     | jq '.choices[0].message.tool_calls'
# A non-null tool_calls array is the pass condition.

PROVIDER_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
PROVIDER_API_KEY=ollama
PROVIDER_MODEL=qwen3.5:9b-64k

# Strip the harness on an explicit `/model qwen` too, not just on failover.
# A deliberate `/model qwen` hits MODEL_ROUTES and skips the failover branch, so
# without this it would arrive unstripped. See `route_bare` in
# src/proxy/config.py.
ROUTE_BARE=true

# Vendor sampling (must match the Modelfile — low temp loops).
PROVIDER_MAX_TOKENS=8192
PROVIDER_TEMPERATURE=1.0
PROVIDER_TOP_P=0.95
PROVIDER_REASONING_EFFORT=none

HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=8082
LOG_FILE=proxy.log

SKIP_QUOTA_PROBES=true
SKIP_TITLE_GENERATION=true
SKIP_SUGGESTION_MODE=true
MOCK_PREFIX_DETECTION=true
MOCK_FILEPATH_EXTRACTION=true

CLAUDE_WORKSPACE=./workspace
MAX_CLI_SESSIONS=5

# Escalate off this tier instead of failing when a session outgrows its window.
# ROUTE_BARE above bounds the prompt, not the transcript, so a long-lived `qwen`
# session walks past the bound on its own; over it, the request goes to the 4B
# 256K tier. See route_max_input_tokens in src/proxy/config.py.
#
# HELD at 28000 across the 27B → 9B step-down even though this tag's window is
# 64K. The bound is a prefill-latency budget, not a window: the 2026-07-09
# 9B → 4B revert happened because a 9B cold-prefilling a large failed-over
# session ran ~5 min/turn and 500'd. Bare mode is what makes 28K safe here.
# Raising this to fill the 64K window re-arms that regression on the same model
# that caused it.
ROUTE_MAX_INPUT_TOKENS=28000
